package main

import ast from "tack/ast"
import builtin from "tack/builtin"
import bytes from "std/bytes"
import codegen from "tack/codegen"
import constant from "tack/constant"
import conv from "std/conv"
import diag from "tack/diag"
import fmt from "std/fmt"
import lexer from "tack/lexer"
import loader from "tack/loader"
import os from "std/os"
import path from "std/path"
import resolver from "tack/resolver"
import source from "tack/source"
import text from "std/text"
import typeck from "tack/typeck"

// Package main is the driver of the Tack compiler written in Tack, the
// tack command. It takes the command line the Go compiler takes and, so
// far, does what that compiler does for these: it answers --backends,
// listing the backends package codegen has; it
// dumps the tokens, --lex, or the syntax trees, --ast, of a single file,
// of the package in a directory, or of the package with an import path
// in the module found from the current directory, or reports their
// errors; for --res it loads the program in full, with the standard
// library, resolves it (see package resolver) and writes the resolved
// tree, a single file's as Go's driver takes it, the first tree of the
// program's first package, and a package's as the whole program, or the
// diagnostics, the loader's and the resolver's together, when any is an
// error, the warnings among them written beside a dump; and for --check,
// without a dump flag, it loads the target as Go's executeCheck does, a
// directory or import path holding no main package as a library (see
// package loader), resolves it with a missing main function allowed and
// reported apart, type-checks it (see package typeck), and reports every
// diagnostic when any stage found an error, exit status 1; and for
// --emit and --emit-header, with --check or without, it does what Go
// does up to code generation: without --check it loads the target as a
// program, with the standard library, resolves it with its main function
// required and type-checks it, and with --check it does what --check
// does, but never compares a copy of the builtins' documentation, as Go
// does not with a dump; when any stage found an error it reports every
// diagnostic, exit status 1, and otherwise it hands the checked program,
// the type checker's handover (see typeck.Typed), to package codegen,
// which rejects, with every diagnostic, a program whose types tack.h
// could not name, as Go's backend does before it lowers anything, and
// whose lowering is not ported yet. --check of std/builtin, or of its
// builtin.tck, compares the copy with the compiler's own, as Go's
// checkBuiltinDoc does. Anything else
// it does not do yet, and says so in the form the differential harness
// in tackc/porttest reads: on standard error,
//
//     tack: not implemented in the port: <what>
//
// and exit status 3, which the harness counts as a skip unless the stage
// is one it requires. --check, --emit and --emit-header decline so when
// no stage before the backend finds an error and tack.h can name every
// type, since Go goes on to lower the program then, which the port does
// not do yet, naming --check, or the dump without it; when the type
// checker meets what it does not check yet; --check when a dump flag
// other than --emit and --emit-header comes with it, or --backends,
// with which Go checks an empty target; and --check without a dump of a
// std/builtin, or its builtin.tck, reached through a symbolic link,
// which Go resolves and the port cannot.
//
// The command line is read as Go's flag package reads it: flags, each
// starting with - or --, come first and stop at the first argument that
// is not one, or after --, and the rest are the inputs. The flags read
// are -lex, -ast, -res, -emit, -emit-header, -check, -backends and the
// -O flags, -O0 to -O3, -Os and -Oz, with one dash or two, and the ones
// that take a value, -max-errors and its old name -max-diags, an int as
// Go's strconv.ParseInt reads it with base 0, -out, a path, and
// -backend, a backend's name, each given after = or as the next
// argument. Any other flag, -lex= with a value among them, -h and -help
// too, is not implemented, named in the line above, as is a build, which
// -out without -check asks for. The usage errors such a command line can
// make are reported as Go's driver reports them, with exit status 2, in
// Go's order: a flag of bad syntax, such as ---lex, a value flag with no
// value or a value that is no int, an error limit below 0, named as the
// flag that set it, -max-diags when it was given; then, unless
// -backends lists the backends, which needs nothing else, two dumps
// asked for, two -O flags, or one with -check or a dump, no input,
// several, an empty name, -out with -check, with a dump, or missing when
// neither is given, and a -backend naming no backend.
//
// The target is loaded as Go's loadWith loads it (see package loader):
// a directory as its package, a name that is no file and looks like an
// import path as one, and anything else as a single file. A file is
// lexed and, for --ast, parsed, the parse stopping past --max-errors
// syntax errors, ten by default, or going on past every one for 0;
// errors are reported, as many at most, followed by a line saying more
// were left out, or the dump written as Go's driver writes it: a
// single file's tokens, or its tree as JSON that decodes to the same
// value (see package ast), several files' tokens each after a header
// line naming the file, and several trees as a list, with Go's
// encoder's refusal of a tree nested too deeply, exit status 1. A
// failure to read what is named is reported as Go reports it, tack: and
// the loader's error, exit status 1, or, when the port cannot word it as
// Go would, as not implemented. The current directory is found as Go's
// os.Getwd finds it, $PWD when it names the same directory as ., and
// getcwd otherwise; the standard library as Go's findStd finds it, from
// $TACK_HOME or the home directory.
//
// --port-loaded is the port's own flag, which the Go compiler does not
// have: it writes what loading in full gives, the program's packages,
// files and import bindings and the diagnostics in the order they were
// reported (see write_loaded), which tackc's porttest compares with what
// the Go loader gives: the directories, C files, error count, import
// bindings and load diagnostics --res does not show, until the builds
// check them. --port-types, the port's too, resolves the program and
// type-checks it as far as the port's checker goes, and writes what it
// finds as flat JSON (see package typeck), which tackc's porttest
// compares with Go's checker run in process; what the checker does not
// do yet is answered as not implemented, naming --port-types. Two more
// are the port's own, for what the backend will need that nothing shows
// until it is ported: --port-floats writes the C literal the backend
// writes for each float a file lists by its bits (see floats_dump), and
// --port-bind what the type table's bindings give a checked program's
// types under each instance's binding (see typeck's write_bind_dump),
// each of which tackc's porttest compares with Go's. --port-header writes
// tack.h for a checked program as the backend renders it, without
// lowering it first as --emit-header does (see header_dump), and
// --port-reserved the names tack.h may not give and the any type ids,
// whatever the target, each of which tackc's porttest compares with
// Go's backend.

// exit_ok means the requested work was done.
var exit_ok int32 = 0

// exit_failure means the program was rejected with diagnostics, or
// reading or writing failed.
var exit_failure int32 = 1

// exit_usage means the command line itself was wrong.
var exit_usage int32 = 2

// not_implemented is the exit status that says the port cannot do what
// the command line asks yet.
var not_implemented int32 = 3

// default_max_errors is how many errors the driver reports when
// --max-errors is not given.
var default_max_errors int64 = 10

// main lists the backends for a command line of --backends alone, runs
// the command line otherwise, and exits with the status run gives. An
// error run returns, which only an allocation that failed gives, is
// written as Go's driver writes an error, tack: and its message, with
// exit status 1.
func main() {
    a, err := os.args(c_allocator)
    if err.code != 0 {
        fmt.eprintf("tack: %s\n", err.msg)
        exit(exit_failure)
    }
    code, e := run(a)
    if e.code != 0 {
        fmt.eprintf("tack: %s\n", e.msg)
        exit(exit_failure)
    }
    exit(code)
}

// unimplemented writes the not implemented line naming what, and returns
// not_implemented, the exit status that goes with it.
func unimplemented(what []uint8) int32 {
    fmt.eprintf("tack: not implemented in the port: %s\n", what)
    return not_implemented
}

// usage writes msg as Go's driver reports a command-line mistake, with a
// pointer to --help, and returns exit_usage.
func usage(msg []uint8) int32 {
    fmt.eprintf("tack: %s\nRun 'tack --help' for usage.\n", msg)
    return exit_usage
}

// Dump is the dump a command line asks for, Go's dump as far as the port
// reads it: --lex, --ast or --res. It wraps a number because Tack
// declares no named integer types.
type Dump struct {
    n uint8
}

// dump_lex is --lex, the token stream.
var dump_lex = Dump{n: 1}

// dump_ast is --ast, the parsed syntax tree.
var dump_ast = Dump{n: 2}

// dump_res is --res, the resolved syntax tree.
var dump_res = Dump{n: 3}

// dump_emit is --emit, the generated C, which the port declines where Go
// would start generating it.
var dump_emit = Dump{n: 4}

// dump_header is --emit-header, tack.h written into the package
// directories, which the port declines where Go would start generating.
var dump_header = Dump{n: 5}

// dump_loaded is --port-loaded, which the Go compiler does not have: the
// program loaded in full, written as a list of its packages (see
// write_loaded), so tackc's porttest can check what the port loads
// that --res does not show.
var dump_loaded = Dump{n: 6}

// dump_types is --port-types, which the Go compiler does not have
// either: what the type checker found, written as flat JSON from the
// handover code generation reads (see package typeck), which tackc's
// porttest compares with what Go's checker finds.
var dump_types = Dump{n: 7}

// dump_floats is --port-floats, the port's too: the C literal the
// backend writes for each float a file lists by its bits (see
// floats_dump), which tackc's porttest compares with Go's.
var dump_floats = Dump{n: 8}

// dump_bind is --port-bind, the port's too: what the type table's
// bindings give a checked program's types under each instance's binding
// (see typeck's write_bind_dump), which tackc's porttest compares with
// Go's Table.
var dump_bind = Dump{n: 9}

// dump_port_header is --port-header, the port's too: tack.h as the
// backend renders it for a checked program, written to standard output
// (see header_dump), which tackc's porttest compares with Go's backend's
// Header run in process. Go's --emit-header lowers the whole program
// before it writes tack.h; this renders it with nothing lowered, so the
// header is compared before the lowering is ported.
var dump_port_header = Dump{n: 10}

// dump_reserved is --port-reserved, the port's too: the names tack.h may
// not give and the any type ids (see codegen.append_reserved_dump),
// which tackc's porttest compares with Go's tables of them.
var dump_reserved = Dump{n: 11}

// dump_flags are the dump flags the port reads, in the order Go's dumpOf
// lists them, which is the order a usage error names them in, each at
// the index of its Dump's number less one; the port's own last.
var dump_flags = [11]string{"--lex", "--ast", "--res", "--emit", "--emit-header", "--port-loaded", "--port-types", "--port-floats", "--port-bind", "--port-header", "--port-reserved"}

// Flags holds what the flags of a command line set, Go's flagValues as
// far as the port reads them: whether each of dump_flags was given,
// --check, --backends, and each -O flag, in codegen.opts' order, whether
// --out names a path, Go's outBin != "", the values of --max-errors and
// --max-diags, and whether --max-diags was given, which makes its value
// the limit, as Go's fs.Visit finds it, and where the value of the last
// --backend is: the index of the argument that holds it, -1 for none,
// and its offset in it.
type Flags struct {
    asked [11]bool
    check bool
    backends bool
    opt [6]bool
    has_out bool
    max_errors int64
    max_diags int64
    diags_set bool
    backend_at int64
    backend_from int64
}

// run carries out the command line a, the program's name and its
// arguments, and returns the exit status: the dump asked for, or the
// check, of the one file, directory or import path named after the
// flags, or a usage error, or not implemented for anything else. The
// flags are read as Go's flag package reads them (see the package
// comment), and then checked in the order Go's parseArgs checks them.
// It returns an error only when an allocation failed.
func run(a []text.Text) (int32, error) {
    // rest holds the index in a of each argument after the flags.
    mut rest := make(list[int64], c_allocator)
    mut asked [11]bool
    mut opt [6]bool
    mut fl := Flags{asked: asked, check: false, backends: false, opt: opt, has_out: false, max_errors: default_max_errors, max_diags: default_max_errors, diags_set: false, backend_at: -1, backend_from: 0}
    mut flags_done := false
    // skip says the argument is the value of the flag before it.
    mut skip := false
    for i, s := range a {
        if i == 0 {
            continue
        }
        if skip {
            skip = false
            continue
        }
        if flags_done {
            append(mut rest, i)?
            continue
        }
        first, has_first := s.bytes()[0]
        if s.len() < 2 || !has_first || first != '-' {
            flags_done = true
            append(mut rest, i)?
            continue
        }
        if s.equal([]uint8("--")) {
            flags_done = true
            continue
        }
        mut start int64 = 1
        second, has_second := s.bytes()[1]
        if has_second && second == '-' {
            start = 2
        }
        c, has := s.bytes()[start]
        if !has || c == '-' || c == '=' {
            mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
            fmt.appendf(mut msg, "bad flag syntax: %s", s.bytes())?
            return usage(msg), noerror
        }
        code, stop, value_next := read_flag(mut fl, a, i, s.bytes(), start)?
        if stop {
            return code, noerror
        }
        skip = value_next
    }
    mut limit := fl.max_errors
    mut limit_name := "--max-errors"
    if fl.diags_set {
        limit = fl.max_diags
        limit_name = "--max-diags"
    }
    if limit < 0 {
        mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        fmt.appendf(mut msg, "%s must be 0 or more, got %d", limit_name, limit)?
        return usage(msg), noerror
    }
    if fl.backends {
        // Listing the backends needs nothing else from the command line,
        // as Go's parseArgs returns there; with --check, Go goes on to
        // check an empty target, which the port does not.
        if fl.check {
            return unimplemented([]uint8("--check")), noerror
        }
        mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        codegen.append_names(mut out, []uint8("\n"))?
        append(mut out, '\n')?
        fmt.write(out)
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    mut count int64 = 0
    mut d := Dump{n: 0}
    mut names := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    for k, on := range fl.asked {
        if !on {
            continue
        }
        if count > 0 {
            append(mut names, []uint8(" and ")...)?
        }
        f, has_f := dump_flags[k]
        if has_f {
            append(mut names, []uint8(f)...)?
        }
        count +%= 1
        n, fits := uint8(k +% 1)
        if fits {
            d = Dump{n: n}
        }
    }
    if count > 1 {
        append(mut names, []uint8(" cannot be combined; pick one")...)?
        return usage(names), noerror
    }
    opt_code, opt_stop := opt_usage(fl, d)?
    if opt_stop {
        return opt_code, noerror
    }
    code, e := command(a, rest, fl, d, limit)
    return code, e
}

// opt_usage checks the -O flags of fl, with the dump d asked for, as Go's
// optOf does, and reports true with the exit status of the usage error
// it writes for two of them, named in codegen.opts' order, or one with
// --check or a dump, which build nothing to optimize.
func opt_usage(fl Flags, d Dump) (int32, bool, error) {
    mut names := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    mut count int64 = 0
    for k, on := range fl.opt {
        if !on {
            continue
        }
        o, has := codegen.opts[k]
        if !has {
            continue
        }
        if count > 0 {
            append(mut names, []uint8(" and ")...)?
        }
        append(mut names, '-', 'O')?
        append(mut names, []uint8(o)...)?
        count +%= 1
    }
    if count > 1 {
        append(mut names, []uint8(" cannot be combined; pick one")...)?
        return usage(names), true, noerror
    }
    if count == 1 && fl.check {
        append(mut names, []uint8(" cannot be combined with --check, which builds nothing")...)?
        return usage(names), true, noerror
    }
    if count == 1 && d.n != 0 {
        append(mut names, []uint8(" cannot be combined with a dump flag, which prints instead of building")...)?
        return usage(names), true, noerror
    }
    return exit_ok, false, noerror
}

// read_flag reads arg, the argument at index i of a, a flag whose name
// starts at start, past its dashes, into fl, as Go's flag package reads
// one: its name runs to the first = after its first byte, and its value,
// when there is an =, from there to the end, or, for a flag that takes
// one and has no =, is the next argument. It returns true, with the
// exit status, when the command line ends here, as a usage error or not
// implemented, and otherwise whether the next argument was taken as the
// value.
func read_flag(mut fl Flags, a []text.Text, i int64, arg []uint8, start int64) (int32, bool, bool, error) {
    mut end := len(arg)
    for j, b := range arg {
        if j > start && b == '=' {
            end = j
            break
        }
    }
    mut vstart := end
    if end < len(arg) {
        vstart = end +% 1
    }
    if start < 0 || start > end || end > len(arg) || vstart < 0 || vstart > len(arg) {
        // start is below len(arg), end at most len(arg) and vstart past
        // end only when end is below len(arg), so this does not happen.
        return unimplemented(arg), true, false, noerror
    }
    code, stop, value_next := read_named(mut fl, a, i, arg, arg[start:end], end < len(arg), arg[vstart:])?
    return code, stop, value_next, noerror
}

// read_named is read_flag for arg, whose name is name and whose value,
// when has_value says it has an =, is value.
func read_named(mut fl Flags, a []text.Text, i int64, arg []uint8, name []uint8, has_value bool, value []uint8) (int32, bool, bool, error) {
    if is_value_flag(name) {
        if has_value {
            code, done := apply_value(mut fl, name, value, i, len(arg) -% len(value))?
            return code, done, false, noerror
        }
        k := i +% 1
        if k < 0 || k >= len(a) {
            mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
            fmt.appendf(mut msg, "flag needs an argument: -%s", name)?
            return usage(msg), true, false, noerror
        }
        code, done := apply_value(mut fl, name, a[k].bytes(), k, 0)?
        return code, done, true, noerror
    }
    if has_value {
        // A value given to a flag that takes none, -lex=true among
        // them, is not read yet.
        return unimplemented(arg), true, false, noerror
    }
    if bytes.equal(name, []uint8("check")) {
        fl.check = true
        return exit_ok, false, false, noerror
    }
    if bytes.equal(name, []uint8("backends")) {
        fl.backends = true
        return exit_ok, false, false, noerror
    }
    for k, o := range codegen.opts {
        if len(name) == 1 +% len(o) && bytes.has_prefix(name, []uint8("O")) && bytes.has_suffix(name, []uint8(o)) && k >= 0 && k < len(fl.opt) {
            fl.opt[k] = true
            return exit_ok, false, false, noerror
        }
    }
    for k, f := range dump_flags {
        if flag_is(name, f) && k >= 0 && k < len(fl.asked) {
            fl.asked[k] = true
            return exit_ok, false, false, noerror
        }
    }
    return unimplemented(arg), true, false, noerror
}

// is_value_flag reports whether name is the name of a flag the port
// reads that takes a value: --max-errors, --max-diags, --out or
// --backend.
func is_value_flag(name []uint8) bool {
    return bytes.equal(name, []uint8("max-errors")) || bytes.equal(name, []uint8("max-diags")) || bytes.equal(name, []uint8("out")) || bytes.equal(name, []uint8("backend"))
}

// apply_value sets the flag name, one is_value_flag accepts, to value,
// in fl, as Go's flag package sets it: --out to a path, which names one
// when it is not empty, --backend to a name, which is the argument at
// index at of the command line from offset on, and --max-errors
// or --max-diags to an int, read as Go's strconv.ParseInt reads it with
// base 0 and 64 bits. A value that is no int is Go's usage error, which
// it writes, returning its exit status and true; otherwise it returns
// false.
func apply_value(mut fl Flags, name []uint8, value []uint8, at int64, offset int64) (int32, bool, error) {
    if bytes.equal(name, []uint8("out")) {
        fl.has_out = len(value) > 0
        return exit_ok, false, noerror
    }
    if bytes.equal(name, []uint8("backend")) {
        fl.backend_at = at
        fl.backend_from = offset
        return exit_ok, false, noerror
    }
    n, err := conv.parse_int(value, 0, 64)
    if err.code != 0 {
        mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        append(mut msg, []uint8("invalid value ")...)?
        conv.append_quote(mut msg, value)?
        fmt.appendf(mut msg, " for flag -%s: ", name)?
        if err.code == 7 {
            append(mut msg, []uint8("value out of range")...)?
        } else {
            append(mut msg, []uint8("parse error")...)?
        }
        return usage(msg), true, noerror
    }
    if bytes.equal(name, []uint8("max-diags")) {
        fl.max_diags = n
        fl.diags_set = true
    } else {
        fl.max_errors = n
    }
    return exit_ok, false, noerror
}

// flag_is reports whether name, a flag's name without its dashes, is
// flag's, written with two dashes.
func flag_is(name []uint8, flag string) bool {
    return len(name) +% 2 == len(flag) && bytes.has_suffix([]uint8(flag), name)
}

// command carries out a command line, a, whose flags set fl and ask for
// the dump d, with the error limit max_errors, and whose arguments
// after the flags are the ones at the indexes rest holds: the one
// input, as Go's fileArg checks it, the flags checked together, as Go's
// checkCombination checks them, and then the check or the dump. A
// build, which the port cannot do, and a check with a dump are not
// implemented.
func command(a []text.Text, rest []int64, fl Flags, d Dump, max_errors int64) (int32, error) {
    if len(rest) == 0 {
        return usage([]uint8("no input file")), noerror
    }
    if len(rest) > 1 {
        mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        fmt.appendf(mut msg, "expected one input file, got %d arguments: ", len(rest))?
        for j, k := range rest {
            if j > 0 {
                append(mut msg, ' ')?
            }
            if k >= 0 && k < len(a) {
                conv.append_quote(mut msg, a[k].bytes())?
            }
        }
        append(mut msg, []uint8(" (flags must come before the file)")...)?
        return usage(msg), noerror
    }
    k := rest[0]
    if k < 0 || k >= len(a) {
        // rest holds indexes of a, so this does not happen.
        return unimplemented([]uint8("(no input)")), noerror
    }
    if a[k].len() == 0 {
        return usage([]uint8("input file name is empty")), noerror
    }
    no_dump := d.n == 0
    if fl.check && fl.has_out {
        return usage([]uint8("--out cannot be combined with --check, which builds nothing")), noerror
    }
    if !fl.check && no_dump && !fl.has_out {
        return usage([]uint8("--out is required to build a binary")), noerror
    }
    if !fl.check && !no_dump && fl.has_out {
        return usage([]uint8("--out cannot be combined with a dump flag, which prints instead of building")), noerror
    }
    bad, bad_code := unknown_backend(a, fl)?
    if bad {
        return bad_code, noerror
    }
    emits := d == dump_emit || d == dump_header
    if fl.check {
        if !no_dump && !emits {
            return unimplemented([]uint8("--check")), noerror
        }
        // With --emit or --emit-header, Go checks the target whatever it
        // is, never comparing a copy of the builtins' documentation.
        code, e := check_target(a[k].bytes(), max_errors, d)
        return code, e
    }
    if no_dump {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--out")), noerror
    }
    if emits {
        code, e := emit_target(a[k].bytes(), d, max_errors)
        return code, e
    }
    if d == dump_floats {
        code, e := floats_dump(a[k].bytes())
        return code, e
    }
    if d == dump_reserved {
        mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        codegen.append_reserved_dump(mut out)?
        fmt.write(out)
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    if d == dump_port_header {
        code, e := header_dump(a[k].bytes(), max_errors)
        return code, e
    }
    code, e := dump_target(a[k].bytes(), d, max_errors)
    return code, e
}

// unknown_backend reports whether the last --backend of fl names no
// backend, writing Go's checkCombination's usage error, with its exit
// status: the name quoted as Go's %q quotes it, and the backends there
// are.
func unknown_backend(a []text.Text, fl Flags) (bool, int32, error) {
    if fl.backend_at < 0 || fl.backend_at >= len(a) {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    bad, code := backend_usage(a[fl.backend_at].bytes(), fl.backend_from)?
    return bad, code, noerror
}

// backend_usage reports whether the name arg holds from offset on names
// no backend, writing Go's usage error for it, with its exit status.
func backend_usage(arg []uint8, offset int64) (bool, int32, error) {
    if offset < 0 || offset > len(arg) {
        // offset is where the value starts in the argument, so this
        // does not happen.
        return true, unimplemented([]uint8("--backend")), noerror
    }
    if codegen.has_backend(arg[offset:]) {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    append(mut msg, []uint8("unknown backend ")...)?
    conv.append_quote(mut msg, arg[offset:])?
    append(mut msg, []uint8("; available: ")...)?
    codegen.append_names(mut msg, []uint8(", "))?
    return true, usage(msg), noerror
}

// flag_name returns the name of the dump flag of d.
func flag_name(d Dump) string {
    if d == dump_ast {
        return "--ast"
    }
    if d == dump_res {
        return "--res"
    }
    if d == dump_loaded {
        return "--port-loaded"
    }
    if d == dump_types {
        return "--port-types"
    }
    if d == dump_emit {
        return "--emit"
    }
    if d == dump_header {
        return "--emit-header"
    }
    if d == dump_floats {
        return "--port-floats"
    }
    if d == dump_bind {
        return "--port-bind"
    }
    if d == dump_port_header {
        return "--port-header"
    }
    if d == dump_reserved {
        return "--port-reserved"
    }
    return "--lex"
}

// failed answers f, a failure Go's driver reports as an error, as Go's
// report does: tack: and its text, with exit status 1; or, for one the
// port cannot word, not implemented, naming the dump d and what f says.
func failed(f loader.Fail, d Dump) (int32, error) {
    if f.unworded {
        code := unimplemented_of(d, f.msg)?
        return code, noerror
    }
    fmt.eprintf("tack: %s\n", f.msg[:])
    return exit_failure, noerror
}

// dump_target writes the dump d of target, with the error limit
// max_errors, as Go's execute does: for
// --res, the standard library is found first, and the target loaded in
// full, as Go's load loads it, and resolved when it is a program (see
// res_dump), or its diagnostics reported; for --lex and --ast, loading stops
// after that stage, having read only the target's own files, and the
// dump is written, or the target's errors. It returns the exit status.
func dump_target(target []uint8, d Dump, max_errors int64) (int32, error) {
    mut l := loader.Loader{
        stop: loader.stage_none,
        max_errors: max_errors,
        builtins: false,
        has_std: false,
        std_dir: text.new(c_allocator),
        relative: false,
        cwd: text.new(c_allocator),
        library: false,
        alloc: c_allocator,
    }
    if d == dump_lex {
        l.stop = loader.stage_lex
    } else if d == dump_ast {
        l.stop = loader.stage_parse
    } else {
        // --res and --port-loaded load in full, with the standard library.
        std_dir, f := find_std()?
        if f.failed {
            code, e := failed(f, d)
            return code, e
        }
        l.std_dir = std_dir
        l.has_std = true
        l.builtins = true
    }
    mut res, single, f := load_with(mut l, target)?
    if d == dump_loaded {
        if f.unworded {
            code, e := failed(f, d)
            return code, e
        }
        out := write_loaded(res, f)?
        fmt.write(out)
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    if f.failed {
        code, e := failed(f, d)
        return code, e
    }
    if d == dump_types || d == dump_bind {
        if res.has_program {
            code, e := types_dump(mut res, d)
            return code, e
        }
        if !diag.has_errors(res.diags) {
            fmt.eprintf("tack: internal error: the loader built no program and reported no error\n")
            return exit_failure, noerror
        }
    }
    if d == dump_res {
        if res.has_program {
            code, e := res_dump(mut res, single, max_errors)
            return code, e
        }
        if !diag.has_errors(res.diags) {
            fmt.eprintf("tack: internal error: the loader built no program and reported no error\n")
            return exit_failure, noerror
        }
    }
    if diag.has_errors(res.diags) {
        out := render_diagnostics(res.files, res.diags, max_errors)?
        fmt.ewrite(out)
        return exit_failure, noerror
    }
    if d == dump_lex {
        mut out := format_file_tokens(res, single)?
        with_trailing_newline(mut out)?
        fmt.write(out)
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    code, e := ast_dump(res, single)
    return code, e
}

// write_loaded returns what --port-loaded writes for res, a load that
// failed with f or did not: error and f's text for a failure; and
// otherwise, for each package of the program, in order, a line package
// with its import path, module, name and directory, a line file with
// the name of each of its files, cfile with the path of each C file,
// and import with each import's path and the index of the package it
// is bound to, -1 for none; then errors and the program's count, or no
// program; and then diag and each diagnostic's file, offset and
// message, in the order they were reported.
func write_loaded(res loader.Result, f loader.Fail) (list[uint8], error) {
    mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    if f.failed {
        fmt.appendf(mut out, "error %s\n", f.msg[:])?
        return out, noerror
    }
    if res.has_program {
        for _, pkg := range res.program.packages {
            fmt.appendf(mut out, "package %s module %s name %s dir %s\n", pkg.path.bytes(), pkg.module.bytes(), pkg.name.bytes(), pkg.dir.bytes())?
            for _, fid := range pkg.file_ids {
                for _, file := range res.files.lookup(fid) {
                    fmt.appendf(mut out, "file %s\n", file.filename.bytes())?
                }
            }
            for _, c := range pkg.c_files {
                fmt.appendf(mut out, "cfile %s\n", c.bytes())?
            }
            mut k int64 = 0
            for _, t := range pkg.files {
                for _, n := range t.imports {
                    switch t.kind(n) {
                    case import_decl(imp):
                        mut index, ok := pkg.imports[k]
                        if !ok {
                            index = -2
                        }
                        fmt.appendf(mut out, "import %s %d\n", t.text(imp.path), index)?
                    default:
                    }
                    k +%= 1
                }
            }
        }
        fmt.appendf(mut out, "errors %d\n", res.program.errors)?
    } else {
        append(mut out, []uint8("no program\n")...)?
    }
    for _, dg := range res.diags {
        for _, file := range res.files.lookup(dg.span.file) {
            fmt.appendf(mut out, "diag %s:%d: %s\n", file.filename.bytes(), dg.span.start, dg.message.bytes())?
        }
    }
    return out, noerror
}

// load_with loads target with l, as Go's loadWith does, and reports
// whether it is a single file: the package in it if it is a directory,
// the package with that import path in the module found from the
// current directory if it names nothing on disk and looks like one, and
// otherwise the one file it names.
func load_with(mut l loader.Loader, target []uint8) (loader.Result, bool, loader.Fail, error) {
    info, stat_err := os.stat(target)
    if stat_err.code == 0 && info.is_dir() {
        res, f := load_dir(mut l, target)?
        return res, false, f, noerror
    }
    if loader.is_enoent(stat_err) && looks_like_import_path(target) {
        res, f := load_path(mut l, target)?
        return res, false, f, noerror
    }
    res, f := l.file(target)?
    return res, true, f, noerror
}

// load_dir loads the directory target with l, Go's loadDir: its module
// is found from the directory's absolute path, and files are shown
// relative to the current directory.
func load_dir(mut l loader.Loader, target []uint8) (loader.Result, loader.Fail, error) {
    abs, af := abs_path(target)?
    if af.failed {
        mut prefix := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        fmt.appendf(mut prefix, "cannot find %s: ", target)?
        w := wrap_fail(prefix, own af)?
        return no_result(), w, noerror
    }
    cwd, cf := go_getwd()?
    if cf.failed {
        w := wrap_fail([]uint8("cannot find the current directory: "), own cf)?
        return no_result(), w, noerror
    }
    l.relative = true
    l.cwd = cwd
    res, f := l.dir(abs.bytes())?
    return res, f, noerror
}

// load_path loads the import path target with l, in the module found
// from the current directory, showing files relative to it, Go's
// loadPath.
func load_path(mut l loader.Loader, target []uint8) (loader.Result, loader.Fail, error) {
    cwd, cf := go_getwd()?
    if cf.failed {
        w := wrap_fail([]uint8("cannot find the current directory: "), own cf)?
        return no_result(), w, noerror
    }
    l.relative = true
    l.cwd = text.from_bytes(cwd.bytes(), c_allocator)?
    res, f := l.path(cwd.bytes(), target)?
    if f.failed {
        mut prefix := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        fmt.appendf(mut prefix, "%s is not a file or a directory, nor an import path: ", target)?
        w := wrap_fail(prefix, own f)?
        return res, w, noerror
    }
    return res, f, noerror
}

// no_result returns the Result of a load that never started, empty.
func no_result() loader.Result {
    l := loader.Loader{
        stop: loader.stage_none,
        max_errors: 0,
        builtins: false,
        has_std: false,
        std_dir: text.new(c_allocator),
        relative: false,
        cwd: text.new(c_allocator),
        library: false,
        alloc: c_allocator,
    }
    return l.empty_result()
}

// wrap_fail returns f with prefix put before its text, as Go's
// fmt.Errorf with %w wraps an error; an unworded failure is returned as
// it is.
func wrap_fail(prefix []uint8, own f loader.Fail) (loader.Fail, error) {
    if f.unworded {
        return f, noerror
    }
    mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    append(mut msg, prefix...)?
    append(mut msg, f.msg...)?
    return loader.Fail{failed: true, unworded: false, not_exist: f.not_exist, no_tack: f.no_tack, msg: msg}, noerror
}

// path_fail returns the failure Go's os package words as op p: and the
// errno's text, for err, std/os's error, or an unworded one, saying
// what, when Go's syscall package's words for its errno are not known.
func path_fail(op string, p []uint8, err error, what string) (loader.Fail, error) {
    errno := loader.go_errno(err)
    mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    if len(errno) == 0 {
        append(mut msg, []uint8(what)...)?
        return loader.Fail{failed: true, unworded: true, not_exist: false, no_tack: false, msg: msg}, noerror
    }
    append(mut msg, []uint8(op)...)?
    if len(p) > 0 {
        append(mut msg, ' ')?
        append(mut msg, p...)?
    }
    append(mut msg, ':', ' ')?
    append(mut msg, []uint8(errno)...)?
    return loader.Fail{failed: true, unworded: false, not_exist: loader.is_enoent(err), no_tack: false, msg: msg}, noerror
}

// go_getwd returns the current directory as Go's os.Getwd gives it on
// Linux: $PWD when it is absolute and names the same file as ., as a
// stat of each says, and getcwd's answer otherwise. A failed stat of .
// is Go's stat .: and the errno, and a failed getcwd getwd: and the
// errno, but for the errnos that make Go look for the directory itself,
// by walking up from ., ENAMETOOLONG and ERANGE among them, which is
// unworded, as is any errno whose Go words are not known.
func go_getwd() (text.Text, loader.Fail, error) {
    none := loader.no_fail(c_allocator)
    pwd, set := os.env([]uint8("PWD"), c_allocator)?
    if set && bytes.has_prefix(pwd.bytes(), []uint8("/")) {
        dot, dot_err := os.stat([]uint8("."))
        if dot_err.code != 0 {
            f := path_fail("stat", []uint8("."), dot_err, " of a current directory that cannot be looked at")?
            return text.new(c_allocator), f, noerror
        }
        here, here_err := os.stat(pwd.bytes())
        if here_err.code == 0 && os.same_file(dot, here) {
            return pwd, none, noerror
        }
    }
    w, err := os.getwd(c_allocator)
    if err.code != 0 {
        if err.code == 3 && (err.msg == "Result too large" || err.msg == "File name too long" || err.msg == "Invalid argument") || err.code == 1 {
            mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
            append(mut msg, []uint8(" in a current directory whose path getcwd cannot give")...)?
            return text.new(c_allocator), loader.Fail{failed: true, unworded: true, not_exist: false, no_tack: false, msg: msg}, noerror
        }
        f := path_fail("getwd", []uint8(""), err, " in a current directory getcwd cannot find")?
        return text.new(c_allocator), f, noerror
    }
    return w, none, noerror
}

// abs_path returns target made absolute, as Go's filepath.Abs does: an
// absolute one cleaned, and a relative one joined to the current
// directory, which go_getwd gives, and cleaned.
func abs_path(target []uint8) (text.Text, loader.Fail, error) {
    if bytes.has_prefix(target, []uint8("/")) {
        t := path.clean(target, c_allocator)?
        return t, loader.no_fail(c_allocator), noerror
    }
    wd, f := go_getwd()?
    if f.failed {
        return text.new(c_allocator), f, noerror
    }
    t := path.join(wd.bytes(), target, c_allocator)?
    return t, f, noerror
}

// find_std finds the standard library as Go's findStd does, for --res,
// and returns its directory, absolute: $TACK_HOME/src/std, or
// ~/.tack/src/std when TACK_HOME is unset or empty, $HOME giving the
// home directory as Go's os.UserHomeDir does. A std that is not there,
// or is not a directory, is a failure saying how to put it there, and
// one that cannot be looked at says why.
func find_std() (text.Text, loader.Fail, error) {
    mut home := text.new(c_allocator)
    tack_home, set := os.env([]uint8("TACK_HOME"), c_allocator)?
    if set && tack_home.len() > 0 {
        mut home.append(tack_home.bytes())?
    } else {
        user, user_set := os.env([]uint8("HOME"), c_allocator)?
        if !user_set || user.len() == 0 {
            mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
            append(mut msg, []uint8("cannot find std: TACK_HOME is not set and the home directory is unknown: $HOME is not defined")...)?
            return text.new(c_allocator), loader.Fail{failed: true, unworded: false, not_exist: false, no_tack: false, msg: msg}, noerror
        }
        home = path.join(user.bytes(), []uint8(".tack"), c_allocator)?
    }
    abs, af := abs_path(home.bytes())?
    if af.failed {
        w := wrap_fail([]uint8("cannot find std: "), own af)?
        return text.new(c_allocator), w, noerror
    }
    src := path.join(abs.bytes(), []uint8("src"), c_allocator)?
    dir := path.join(src.bytes(), []uint8("std"), c_allocator)?
    info, err := os.stat(dir.bytes())
    if loader.is_enoent(err) || err.code == 0 && !info.is_dir() {
        mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        fmt.appendf(mut msg, "cannot find std at %s; set TACK_HOME or run make install", dir.bytes())?
        return text.new(c_allocator), loader.Fail{failed: true, unworded: false, not_exist: false, no_tack: false, msg: msg}, noerror
    }
    if err.code != 0 {
        f := path_fail("stat", dir.bytes(), err, " of a standard library that cannot be looked at")?
        mut prefix := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        fmt.appendf(mut prefix, "cannot read std at %s: ", dir.bytes())?
        w := wrap_fail(prefix, own f)?
        return text.new(c_allocator), w, noerror
    }
    return dir, loader.no_fail(c_allocator), noerror
}

// res_dump runs the resolver on the program res holds and writes what
// --res writes, as Go's runStages and compile do: every diagnostic, the
// loader's and the resolver's, when any is an error, exit status 1; and
// otherwise the warnings, on standard error, and the resolved tree, the
// first tree of the first package for a single file, as Go's
// resolvedDump takes it, and the whole program otherwise, or Go's
// encoder's refusal of a tree nested too deeply, the diagnostics
// rendered with the error limit max_errors. It returns the exit status.
func res_dump(mut res loader.Result, single bool, max_errors int64) (int32, error) {
    prog := program_of(mut res.program.packages, res.program.errors)?
    resolved, mut diags := resolver.resolve(own prog, builtin.default_set(), false)?
    mut all := replace(mut res.diags, make(list[diag.Diagnostic], c_allocator))
    for i := range len(diags) {
        append(mut all, replace(mut diags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
    }
    if diag.has_errors(all) {
        out := render_diagnostics(res.files, all, max_errors)?
        fmt.ewrite(out)
        return exit_failure, noerror
    }
    mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    mut refused := ""
    if single {
        for i, t := range resolved.trees {
            if i == 0 {
                o, rf := ast.dump(t, res.files, resolved.trees, c_allocator)?
                out = o
                refused = rf
            }
        }
    } else {
        o, rf := ast.dump_program(resolved, res.files, c_allocator)?
        out = o
        refused = rf
    }
    if len(refused) > 0 {
        fmt.eprintf("tack: failed to render syntax tree: %s\n", refused)
        return exit_failure, noerror
    }
    if len(all) > 0 {
        w := render_diagnostics(res.files, all, max_errors)?
        fmt.ewrite(w)
    }
    with_trailing_newline(mut out)?
    fmt.write(out)
    return exit_ok, noerror
}

// types_dump writes the --port-types dump of the program res holds:
// resolved, then checked as far as the type checker goes (see package
// typeck), whatever the stages before reported, as Go's checker runs
// whatever they reported, and its findings written as flat JSON (see
// typeck's check_types): when only the function bodies are beyond the
// port's checker, what its declaration phase found; what the checker
// does not do yet before then is answered as not implemented.
//
// For --port-bind, d, the program is checked in full, and what the
// type table's bindings give under each instance's is written (see
// typeck's write_bind_dump); a check that stops short of the bodies, or
// a binding the port declines, is not implemented, naming --port-bind.
func types_dump(mut res loader.Result, d Dump) (int32, error) {
    prog := program_of(mut res.program.packages, res.program.errors)?
    resolved, mut diags := resolver.resolve(own prog, builtin.default_set(), false)?
    drop(own diags)
    files := replace(mut res.files, source.new_set(c_allocator))
    mut c := typeck.new_checker(own resolved, own files)?
    if d == dump_bind {
        ce := mut c.check()
        if ce.code == typeck.not_ported.code {
            return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-bind")), noerror
        }
        if ce.code != 0 {
            return exit_failure, ce
        }
        mut t := mut c.take_typed()?
        mut bout := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        be := mut t.write_bind_dump(mut bout)
        if be.code == typeck.not_ported.code {
            return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-bind")), noerror
        }
        if be.code != 0 {
            return exit_failure, be
        }
        fmt.write(bout)
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    e := mut c.check_types()
    if e.code == typeck.not_ported.code {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-types")), noerror
    }
    if e.code != 0 {
        return exit_failure, e
    }
    out := mut c.write_dump()?
    fmt.write(out)
    return exit_ok, noerror
}

// emit_target carries out --emit or --emit-header, d, of target, with
// the error limit max_errors, as Go's execute and runStages do: the
// standard library is found, the target loaded in full as a program,
// resolved with its main function required, and type-checked, whatever
// the stages before reported; when any stage reported an error, every
// diagnostic is rendered, exit status 1, and otherwise the checked
// program is handed to code generation, where Go would generate the C,
// which the port declines, naming d. A program the type checker cannot
// check yet is declined too, as a failure to read the target the port
// cannot word.
func emit_target(target []uint8, d Dump, max_errors int64) (int32, error) {
    std_dir, sf := find_std()?
    if sf.failed {
        code, e := failed(sf, d)
        return code, e
    }
    mut l := loader.Loader{
        stop: loader.stage_none,
        max_errors: max_errors,
        builtins: true,
        has_std: true,
        std_dir: std_dir,
        relative: false,
        cwd: text.new(c_allocator),
        library: false,
        alloc: c_allocator,
    }
    mut res, _, f := load_with(mut l, target)?
    if f.failed {
        code, e := failed(f, d)
        return code, e
    }
    mut all := replace(mut res.diags, make(list[diag.Diagnostic], c_allocator))
    if !res.has_program {
        if !diag.has_errors(all) {
            fmt.eprintf("tack: internal error: the loader built no program and reported no error\n")
            return exit_failure, noerror
        }
        out := render_diagnostics(res.files, all, max_errors)?
        fmt.ewrite(out)
        return exit_failure, noerror
    }
    prog := program_of(mut res.program.packages, res.program.errors)?
    resolved, mut rdiags := resolver.resolve(own prog, builtin.default_set(), false)?
    for i := range len(rdiags) {
        append(mut all, replace(mut rdiags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
    }
    files := replace(mut res.files, source.new_set(c_allocator))
    mut c := typeck.new_checker(own resolved, own files)?
    e := mut c.check()
    if e.code == typeck.not_ported.code {
        return unimplemented([]uint8(flag_name(d))), noerror
    }
    if e.code != 0 {
        return exit_failure, e
    }
    mut tdiags := mut c.take_diags()?
    for i := range len(tdiags) {
        append(mut all, replace(mut tdiags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
    }
    if diag.has_errors(all) {
        checked, back := mut c.take_program()
        drop(own checked)
        out := render_diagnostics(back, all, max_errors)?
        fmt.ewrite(out)
        return exit_failure, noerror
    }
    mut typed := mut c.take_typed()?
    gout, mut gdiags, ge := codegen.generate(mut typed, builtin.default_set())
    if ge.code == codegen.not_ported.code {
        return unimplemented([]uint8(flag_name(d))), noerror
    }
    if ge.code != 0 {
        return exit_failure, ge
    }
    if diag.has_errors(gdiags) {
        // What the backend reports, Go's compile appends after the
        // warnings of the stages before, and renders with them.
        for i := range len(gdiags) {
            append(mut all, replace(mut gdiags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
        }
        out := render_diagnostics(typed.files, all, max_errors)?
        fmt.ewrite(out)
        return exit_failure, noerror
    }
    // Go's compileWarned writes the warnings, then the dump is written.
    for i := range len(gdiags) {
        append(mut all, replace(mut gdiags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
    }
    if len(all) > 0 {
        out := render_diagnostics(typed.files, all, max_errors)?
        fmt.ewrite(out)
    }
    if d == dump_header {
        hcode := write_headers(mut typed, d)?
        return hcode, noerror
    }
    mut emitted := gout
    with_trailing_newline(mut emitted)?
    fmt.write(emitted)
    return exit_ok, noerror
}

// write_headers writes tack.h, the header codegen renders for t's
// program, into the directory of its main package and of every other
// package of its module with C files or extern funcs, Go's writeHeaders:
// a library's main package only when it has either, and no package of
// another module. A file that cannot be written, whose error Go words
// with the operating system's, is not implemented, naming the dump d.
func write_headers(mut t typeck.Typed, d Dump) (int32, error) {
    header := codegen.header(mut t)?
    code := write_header_files(t.prog, header[:], d)?
    return code, noerror
}

// write_header_files writes header, prog's tack.h, where write_headers
// says, naming d when a file cannot be written.
func write_header_files(prog ast.Program, header []uint8, d Dump) (int32, error) {
    main := len(prog.packages) -% 1
    if main < 0 || main >= len(prog.packages) {
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    library := !prog.packages[main].name.equal([]uint8("main"))
    for p := range len(prog.packages) {
        if !header_wanted(prog, p, main, library) {
            continue
        }
        name := path.join(prog.packages[p].dir.bytes(), []uint8(codegen.header_name), c_allocator)?
        we := os.write_file(name.bytes(), header)
        if we.code != 0 {
            return unimplemented([]uint8(flag_name(d))), noerror
        }
    }
    return exit_ok, noerror
}

// header_wanted reports whether write_headers writes tack.h into the
// package p of prog, main being its main package and library whether
// the program is a library.
func header_wanted(prog ast.Program, p int64, main int64, library bool) bool {
    if p < 0 || p >= len(prog.packages) || main < 0 || main >= len(prog.packages) {
        return false
    }
    if !library && p == main {
        return true
    }
    if !prog.packages[p].module.equal(prog.packages[main].module.bytes()) {
        return false
    }
    if len(prog.packages[p].c_files) > 0 {
        return true
    }
    first := prog.packages[p].first
    for u := range prog.packages[p].count {
        unit := first +% u
        if unit < 0 || unit >= len(prog.trees) {
            continue
        }
        for _, n := range prog.trees[unit].decls {
            switch prog.trees[unit].nodes[n].k {
            case func_decl(fid):
                if prog.trees[unit].funcs[fid].is_extern {
                    return true
                }
            default:
            }
        }
    }
    return false
}

// header_dump writes what --port-header writes for target: the standard
// library is found and the target loaded as --check loads it, a library
// when it holds no main package, resolved with a missing main allowed,
// and type-checked; when no stage reported an error, tack.h as the
// backend's header renders it, written to standard output, whatever
// header_diags finds, as Go's Header renders it whatever headerDiags
// finds, and exit status 0. A program with an error, and one the type
// checker cannot check yet, is not implemented, naming --port-header:
// tack.h is only ever written for a program with none.
func header_dump(target []uint8, max_errors int64) (int32, error) {
    std_dir, sf := find_std()?
    if sf.failed {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-header")), noerror
    }
    mut l := loader.Loader{
        stop: loader.stage_none,
        max_errors: max_errors,
        builtins: true,
        has_std: true,
        std_dir: std_dir,
        relative: false,
        cwd: text.new(c_allocator),
        library: true,
        alloc: c_allocator,
    }
    mut res, _, f := load_with(mut l, target)?
    if f.failed || !res.has_program || diag.has_errors(res.diags) {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-header")), noerror
    }
    prog := program_of(mut res.program.packages, res.program.errors)?
    resolved, rdiags := resolver.resolve(own prog, builtin.default_set(), true)?
    if diag.has_errors(rdiags) {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-header")), noerror
    }
    files := replace(mut res.files, source.new_set(c_allocator))
    mut c := typeck.new_checker(own resolved, own files)?
    e := mut c.check()
    if e.code == typeck.not_ported.code {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-header")), noerror
    }
    if e.code != 0 {
        return exit_failure, e
    }
    tdiags := mut c.take_diags()?
    if diag.has_errors(tdiags) {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-header")), noerror
    }
    mut typed := mut c.take_typed()?
    out := codegen.header(mut typed)?
    fmt.write(out)
    return exit_ok, noerror
}

// floats_dump writes what --port-floats writes for the file target: for
// each of its lines, 64 or 32, a space and the bits of a float64 or a
// float32 in lowercase hexadecimal, the C literal of the float constant
// they are, as the C backend writes it (see
// constant.append_float_literal), one a line. A line it cannot read, or
// a file it cannot, is not implemented, naming --port-floats.
func floats_dump(target []uint8) (int32, error) {
    data, err := os.read_file(target, c_allocator)
    if err.code != 0 {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-floats")), noerror
    }
    mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    mut start int64 = 0
    for start < len(data) {
        mut end := start
        for end < len(data) {
            b, ok := data[end]
            if !ok || b == '\n' {
                break
            }
            end +%= 1
        }
        if end > start && start >= 0 && end <= len(data) {
            ok := float_line(data[start:end], mut out)?
            if !ok {
                return unimplemented([]uint8("--port-floats")), noerror
            }
        }
        start = end +% 1
    }
    fmt.write(out)
    return exit_ok, noerror
}

// float_line appends to out the literal of the float line names, as
// floats_dump reads it, and a newline, and reports whether it could read
// the line.
func float_line(line []uint8, mut out list[uint8]) (bool, error) {
    is32 := bytes.has_prefix(line, []uint8("32 "))
    if !is32 && !bytes.has_prefix(line, []uint8("64 ")) || len(line) < 4 {
        return false, noerror
    }
    mut bits uint64 = 0
    for i, b := range line {
        if i < 3 {
            continue
        }
        mut v uint64 = 16
        if b >= '0' && b <= '9' {
            v = uint64(b -% '0')
        } else if b >= 'a' && b <= 'f' {
            v = uint64(b -% 'a' +% 10)
        }
        if v > 15 || bits >> 60 != 0 {
            return false, noerror
        }
        bits = bits << 4 | v
    }
    constant.append_float_literal(mut out, bits, is32)?
    append(mut out, '\n')?
    return true, noerror
}

// check_failed answers f, a failure Go's driver reports as an error,
// for --check, as failed does for a dump: tack: and its text, exit
// status 1, or, for one the port cannot word, not implemented, naming
// --check and what f says.
func check_failed(f loader.Fail) (int32, error) {
    if f.unworded {
        mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
        append(mut msg, []uint8("--check")...)?
        append(mut msg, f.msg...)?
        return unimplemented(msg), noerror
    }
    fmt.eprintf("tack: %s\n", f.msg[:])
    return exit_failure, noerror
}

// check_target carries out --check, without a dump, for target, with
// the error limit max_errors, as Go's executeCheck does: the standard
// library is found, the target loaded in full as a library when it is a
// directory or import path holding no main package (see loader.Loader's
// lib), and the program checked (see check_program). A target that may
// be std/builtin or its builtin.tck, which Go compares with the
// compiler's own copy instead, is compared with it (see check_builtin_doc).
//
// d is the dump the check writes, none, --emit or --emit-header: Go
// looks for that copy without a dump; with --emit and --emit-header it
// does not, checking the target as it is.
func check_target(target []uint8, max_errors int64, d Dump) (int32, error) {
    doc := d.n == 0
    std_dir, sf := find_std()?
    if sf.failed {
        code, e := check_failed(sf)
        return code, e
    }
    mut l := loader.Loader{
        stop: loader.stage_none,
        max_errors: max_errors,
        builtins: true,
        has_std: true,
        std_dir: std_dir,
        relative: false,
        cwd: text.new(c_allocator),
        library: true,
        alloc: c_allocator,
    }
    if doc {
        handled, doc_code := check_builtin_doc(target, l)?
        if handled {
            return doc_code, noerror
        }
    }
    mut res, _, f := load_with(mut l, target)?
    if f.failed {
        code, e := check_failed(f)
        return code, e
    }
    code, e := check_program(mut res, max_errors, d)
    return code, e
}

// check_builtin_doc checks target when it is std/builtin/builtin.tck, or
// the directory std/builtin, and reports whether it was, with the exit
// status, Go's checkBuiltinDoc, with l the loader that finds the module
// std: that file is a copy of the builtins' documentation, which is
// never checked as code. It is clean, exit status 0 and nothing written,
// when it matches the compiler's own, builtin.doc, and an error saying
// how to refresh it when it does not. Any other target is left to the
// loader, and so is a file whose name as given makes it a test file, and
// a target that cannot be looked at. Go resolves the symbolic links of
// the target's path first; the port cannot resolve a link, so a target
// that may still be the copy, a directory holding a builtin.tck, a file
// named builtin.tck or a link, with a link among the parts of its path,
// made absolute as Go's filepath.Abs makes it, is not implemented,
// naming --check.
func check_builtin_doc(target []uint8, l loader.Loader) (bool, int32, error) {
    abs, af := abs_path(target)?
    if af.failed {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    info, err := os.stat(abs.bytes())
    if err.code != 0 {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    base := path.base(abs.bytes(), c_allocator)?
    if !info.is_dir() && loader.is_test_file(base.bytes()) {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    // What no link can change comes first, so that only a target that
    // may still be the copy is declined for a link on its path: a
    // directory holding no builtin.tck, which stat finds through any
    // link, and a file that is no link itself, whose name, which Go
    // resolves no further, is not builtin.tck.
    if info.is_dir() {
        doc := path.join(abs.bytes(), []uint8("builtin.tck"), c_allocator)?
        _, doc_err := os.stat(doc.bytes())
        if doc_err.code != 0 {
            return false, exit_ok, noerror
        }
    } else if !base.equal([]uint8("builtin.tck")) && !is_link(abs.bytes()) {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    linked := has_link(abs.bytes())?
    if linked {
        return true, unimplemented([]uint8("--check")), noerror
    }
    mut file := text.new(c_allocator)
    mut dir := text.new(c_allocator)
    if info.is_dir() {
        file = path.join(abs.bytes(), []uint8("builtin.tck"), c_allocator)?
        dir = text.from_bytes(abs.bytes(), c_allocator)?
    } else {
        if !base.equal([]uint8("builtin.tck")) {
            return false, exit_ok, noerror
        }
        file = text.from_bytes(abs.bytes(), c_allocator)?
        dir = path.dir(abs.bytes(), c_allocator)?
    }
    is_std := l.is_std_builtin_dir(dir.bytes())?
    if !is_std {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    data, rerr := os.read_file(file.bytes(), c_allocator)
    if rerr.code != 0 {
        return false, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    if bytes.equal(data[:], []uint8(builtin.doc)) {
        return true, exit_ok, noerror
    }
    fmt.eprintf("tack: %s differs from the builtins built into the compiler; refresh it with make std-copies, or make install-std for an installed std\n", target)
    return true, exit_failure, noerror
}

// has_link reports whether any part of the absolute path abs, from its
// first directory to itself, is a symbolic link (see is_link).
func has_link(abs []uint8) (bool, error) {
    for i, b := range abs {
        if i == 0 || b != '/' && i +% 1 != len(abs) {
            continue
        }
        mut end := i
        if b != '/' {
            end = i +% 1
        }
        if end <= 0 || end > len(abs) {
            continue
        }
        if is_link(abs[:end]) {
            return true, noerror
        }
    }
    return false, noerror
}

// is_link reports whether p is a symbolic link: one whose lstat and stat
// name different files, or whose stat fails, a link that leads nowhere.
// A p that cannot be looked at is none.
func is_link(p []uint8) bool {
    link, lerr := os.lstat(p)
    if lerr.code != 0 {
        return false
    }
    info, serr := os.stat(p)
    return serr.code != 0 || !os.same_file(link, info)
}

// check_program checks the program res holds, loaded in full, with the
// error limit max_errors, as Go's checkProgram and executeCheck do. No
// program leaves the loader's diagnostics, rendered, exit status 1 when
// one is an error, and otherwise written as warnings, exit status 0. A
// program is resolved with a missing main function allowed, which is
// reported apart (see resolver.missing_main), and then type-checked;
// when the loader, the resolver or the type checker found an error,
// the missing main and every diagnostic are rendered, exit status 1.
// Otherwise Go goes on to the C backend, which the port does not have,
// so the check is not implemented, as is a program the type checker
// cannot check yet.
func check_program(mut res loader.Result, max_errors int64, d Dump) (int32, error) {
    mut all := replace(mut res.diags, make(list[diag.Diagnostic], c_allocator))
    if !res.has_program {
        if len(all) > 0 {
            out := render_diagnostics(res.files, all, max_errors)?
            fmt.ewrite(out)
        }
        if diag.has_errors(all) {
            return exit_failure, noerror
        }
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    prog := program_of(mut res.program.packages, res.program.errors)?
    mut missing := resolver.missing_main(prog)?
    resolved, mut rdiags := resolver.resolve(own prog, builtin.default_set(), true)?
    for i := range len(rdiags) {
        append(mut all, replace(mut rdiags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
    }
    files := replace(mut res.files, source.new_set(c_allocator))
    mut c := typeck.new_checker(own resolved, own files)?
    e := mut c.check()
    if e.code == typeck.not_ported.code {
        return unimplemented([]uint8("--check")), noerror
    }
    if e.code != 0 {
        return exit_failure, e
    }
    mut tdiags := mut c.take_diags()?
    for i := range len(tdiags) {
        append(mut all, replace(mut tdiags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
    }
    if !diag.has_errors(all) {
        // Go runs the C backend here, whatever the missing main.
        mut typed := mut c.take_typed()?
        gout, mut gdiags, ge := codegen.generate(mut typed, builtin.default_set())
        if ge.code == codegen.not_ported.code {
            return unimplemented([]uint8("--check")), noerror
        }
        if ge.code != 0 {
            return exit_failure, ge
        }
        if len(gdiags) > 0 || len(missing) > 0 {
            // Go's checkProgram puts the missing main first, then what
            // the backend reported, then the stages' warnings.
            for i := range len(gdiags) {
                append(mut missing, replace(mut gdiags[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
            }
            for i := range len(all) {
                append(mut missing, replace(mut all[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
            }
            out := render_diagnostics(typed.files, missing, max_errors)?
            fmt.ewrite(out)
            if diag.has_errors(missing) {
                return exit_failure, noerror
            }
            return exit_ok, noerror
        }
        // Go's writeCheckOutput writes tack.h, or the C, then the
        // warnings follow.
        if d == dump_header {
            hcode := write_headers(mut typed, d)?
            if hcode != exit_ok {
                return hcode, noerror
            }
        }
        if d == dump_emit {
            mut emitted := gout
            with_trailing_newline(mut emitted)?
            fmt.write(emitted)
        }
        if len(all) > 0 {
            out := render_diagnostics(typed.files, all, max_errors)?
            fmt.ewrite(out)
        }
        return exit_ok, noerror
    }
    checked, back := mut c.take_program()
    drop(own checked)
    for i := range len(all) {
        append(mut missing, replace(mut all[i], make(diag.Diagnostic, c_allocator)))?
    }
    out := render_diagnostics(back, missing, max_errors)?
    fmt.ewrite(out)
    return exit_failure, noerror
}

// program_of returns the program the loader built, of packages, taken
// out of them, with its error count errors, as the
// resolver takes it: every tree of every package in one list, in the
// packages' order, each package naming its run of them.
func program_of(mut packages list[loader.Package], errors int64) (ast.Program, error) {
    mut prog := ast.new_program(c_allocator)
    prog.errors = errors
    for i := range len(packages) {
        mut pkg := ast.Package{
            name: replace(mut packages[i].name, text.new(c_allocator)),
            path: replace(mut packages[i].path, text.new(c_allocator)),
            module: replace(mut packages[i].module, text.new(c_allocator)),
            dir: replace(mut packages[i].dir, text.new(c_allocator)),
            c_files: replace(mut packages[i].c_files, make(list[text.Text], c_allocator)),
            first: len(prog.trees),
            count: 0,
            imports: replace(mut packages[i].imports, make(list[int64], c_allocator)),
        }
        mut trees := replace(mut packages[i].files, make(list[ast.File], c_allocator))
        for j := range len(trees) {
            append(mut prog.trees, replace(mut trees[j], ast.new_file(c_allocator)))?
            pkg.count +%= 1
        }
        append(mut prog.packages, pkg)?
    }
    return prog, noerror
}

// ast_dump writes the --ast dump of what res, loaded to that stage,
// holds, as Go's driver writes it: a single file's tree as it is, and
// several files' trees as a list, or Go's encoder's refusal of a tree
// nested too deeply, exit status 1. It returns the exit status.
func ast_dump(res loader.Result, single bool) (int32, error) {
    mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    mut refused := ""
    if single {
        for i, t := range res.trees {
            if i == 0 {
                o, r := ast.dump(t, res.files, res.trees[:0], c_allocator)?
                out = o
                refused = r
            }
        }
    } else {
        o, r := ast.dump_list(res.trees, res.files, c_allocator)?
        out = o
        refused = r
    }
    if len(refused) > 0 {
        fmt.eprintf("tack: failed to render syntax tree: %s\n", refused)
        return exit_failure, noerror
    }
    with_trailing_newline(mut out)?
    fmt.write(out)
    return exit_ok, noerror
}

// format_file_tokens renders the tokens of every file res holds, as Go's
// formatFileTokens does: a single file's alone, and each of several after
// a header line naming its file, -- name --.
func format_file_tokens(res loader.Result, single bool) (list[uint8], error) {
    mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    for i, ts := range res.tokens {
        if single {
            if i == 0 {
                out = format_tokens(ts)?
            }
            continue
        }
        fid, ok := res.sources[i]
        if ok {
            for _, f := range res.files.lookup(fid) {
                fmt.appendf(mut out, "-- %s --\n", f.filename.bytes())?
            }
        }
        part := format_tokens(ts)?
        append(mut out, part...)?
    }
    return out, noerror
}

// unimplemented_of writes the not implemented line naming the dump flag
// of d followed by what, and returns not_implemented.
func unimplemented_of(d Dump, what []uint8) (int32, error) {
    mut msg := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    append(mut msg, []uint8(flag_name(d))...)?
    append(mut msg, what...)?
    return unimplemented(msg), noerror
}

// looks_like_import_path reports whether target, which names nothing on
// disk, could be an import path rather than a mistyped file: it is not
// absolute, does not start with a dot, and does not end in .tck.
func looks_like_import_path(target []uint8) bool {
    return len(target) > 0 && !bytes.has_prefix(target, []uint8("/")) &&
        !bytes.has_prefix(target, []uint8(".")) && !bytes.has_suffix(target, []uint8(".tck"))
}

// render_diagnostics renders diags, problems in the files of files,
// sorted by file and then by where each starts, up to the error past the
// first max_errors when max_errors is positive, followed, when that left
// some out, by a line saying so (see too_many_errors). Only errors count
// toward max_errors: the warnings among the errors shown are shown too,
// and those after the last error shown are left out with it, as the
// errors after it are. Go sorts a clone of the diagnostics; a Diagnostic
// is owned and cannot be cloned, so this sorts their indexes instead,
// stably, and renders them in that order as Go's diag.RenderAllPretty
// renders a list: a blank line, then each diagnostic followed by one.
func render_diagnostics(files source.Set, diags []diag.Diagnostic, max_errors int64) (list[uint8], error) {
    mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    mut order := make(list[int64], c_allocator)
    for i := range len(diags) {
        append(mut order, i)?
    }
    sort_diagnostics(files, diags, mut order)?

    mut shown := len(order)
    mut more := false
    if max_errors > 0 {
        mut errs int64 = 0
        for j, k := range order {
            if k < 0 || k >= len(diags) || diags[k].severity != diag.error_severity {
                continue
            }
            // errs counts diagnostics, so it never wraps.
            errs +%= 1
            if errs > max_errors {
                shown = j
                more = true
                break
            }
        }
    }

    append(mut out, '\n')?
    for j, k := range order {
        if j >= shown {
            break
        }
        if k >= 0 && k < len(diags) {
            diag.render_pretty(files, mut out, diags[k])?
            append(mut out, '\n')?
        }
    }
    if more {
        too_many_errors(mut out, max_errors)?
    }
    return out, noerror
}

// too_many_errors appends the line that ends the report of the first n
// errors when there were more.
func too_many_errors(mut out list[uint8], n int64) error {
    return fmt.appendf(mut out, "too many errors: showing the first %d; --max-errors 0 shows them all\n", n)
}

// diag_less reports whether diagnostic i of diags sorts before diagnostic
// j: by the name of the file its span points into, a file no Set holds
// sorting as an empty name, and then by where the span starts. Go
// compares the spans' FileIDs, which are the files' names there; here a
// FileID is an index, so the names are looked up.
func diag_less(files source.Set, diags []diag.Diagnostic, i int64, j int64) bool {
    if i < 0 || i >= len(diags) || j < 0 || j >= len(diags) {
        return false
    }
    a := diags[i].span
    b := diags[j].span
    if a.file != b.file {
        c := compare_names(files, a.file, b.file)
        if c != 0 {
            return c < 0
        }
    }
    return a.start < b.start
}

// compare_names compares the names of the files a and b of files,
// bytewise, as Go's cmp.Compare compares two strings: -1 when a's sorts
// first, 1 when b's does, and 0 when they are equal. A FileID no file of
// files has stands for an empty name.
func compare_names(files source.Set, a source.FileID, b source.FileID) int64 {
    for _, fa := range files.lookup(a) {
        for _, fb := range files.lookup(b) {
            return compare_bytes(fa.filename.bytes(), fb.filename.bytes())
        }
        if fa.filename.len() > 0 {
            return 1
        }
        return 0
    }
    for _, fb := range files.lookup(b) {
        if fb.filename.len() > 0 {
            return -1
        }
    }
    return 0
}

// compare_bytes compares a and b bytewise: -1 when a sorts first, a
// prefix of b included, 1 when b does, and 0 when they are equal.
func compare_bytes(a []uint8, b []uint8) int64 {
    for i, x := range a {
        y, ok := b[i]
        if !ok {
            return 1
        }
        if x != y {
            if x < y {
                return -1
            }
            return 1
        }
    }
    if len(a) < len(b) {
        return -1
    }
    return 0
}

// sort_diagnostics sorts order, indexes into diags, by diag_less, keeping
// the order of indexes that compare equal, as Go's slices.SortStableFunc
// does: a merge sort, bottom up, so it needs no recursion, merging runs
// of width 1, 2, 4 and on from one list into a second.
func sort_diagnostics(files source.Set, diags []diag.Diagnostic, mut order list[int64]) error {
    n := len(order)
    mut width int64 = 1
    for width < n {
        mut to := make(list[int64], c_allocator)
        reserve(mut to, n)?
        mut lo int64 = 0
        for lo < n {
            // lo, width and n are all at most len(order), so none of
            // the sums below comes near wrapping.
            mut mid := lo +% width
            if mid > n {
                mid = n
            }
            mut hi := mid +% width
            if hi > n {
                hi = n
            }
            merge(files, diags, order, lo, mid, hi, mut to)?
            lo = hi
        }
        order = to
        width = width *% 2
    }
    return noerror
}

// merge appends to to the runs src[lo:mid] and src[mid:hi], each already
// sorted by diag_less, merged into one sorted run. An element of the
// second run goes first only when it sorts strictly before the first
// run's, which keeps the sort stable.
func merge(files source.Set, diags []diag.Diagnostic, src []int64, lo int64, mid int64, hi int64, mut to list[int64]) error {
    mut i := lo
    mut j := mid
    for i < mid || j < hi {
        a, a_ok := src[i]
        b, b_ok := src[j]
        if i < mid && a_ok && (j >= hi || !b_ok || !diag_less(files, diags, b, a)) {
            append(mut to, a)?
            i +%= 1
        } else if j < hi && b_ok {
            append(mut to, b)?
            j +%= 1
        } else {
            // Both runs are within src, so this does not happen; it ends
            // the loop rather than spin should it.
            break
        }
    }
    return noerror
}

// format_tokens renders the tokens of ts one per line, with every offset
// zero-padded to the width of the largest one so the columns line up, as
// Go's formatTokens does. The largest offset is the end of the last
// token, since tokens are in source order. No tokens render as nothing.
func format_tokens(ts lexer.Tokens) (list[uint8], error) {
    mut out := make(list[uint8], c_allocator)
    last, ok := ts.tokens()[len(ts.tokens()) -% 1]
    if !ok {
        return out, noerror
    }
    // width is the number of digits of the last token's end, or 1 when
    // that is not positive, as Go's len(strconv.Itoa(max(end, 0))) is.
    mut width int64 = 1
    mut rest := last.offset_end() / 10
    for rest > 0 {
        width +%= 1
        rest = rest / 10
    }
    for _, t := range ts.tokens() {
        lexer.append_padded(mut out, t, ts.value(t), width)?
        append(mut out, '\n')?
    }
    return out, noerror
}

// with_trailing_newline adds a newline to the end of b, a dump about to
// be written, only if b is non-empty and lacks it, so a dump ends cleanly
// on a terminal without doubling the newline generated code already ends
// with.
func with_trailing_newline(mut b list[uint8]) error {
    if len(b) > 0 && !bytes.has_suffix(b, []uint8("\n")) {
        return append(mut b, '\n')
    }
    return noerror
}
